A||||9||btoth||CD-R question||||5||
Z||000000||btoth||11-01-2001||03:46 PM||||OK, I've always wondered:  what's different about those CD-Rs marked for "audio" or "music".  Do they conform better to regular CD standards or something?  Or is it just a gimmick?<P>I know some brands of CD-Rs do make crappy audio CDs.  (Like these Imation ones that don't work in my car or home CD player, but work in my computer CD-ROM).<P>Are they better quality?<P>I remember when the CD-writers first came out you had three choices of CD-R quality:  Gold, Silver, and the Blue/Green ones.  Now all you can find are the blue ones.  Are the "audio/music" ones different somehow?||||reg||5
Z||000001||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-01-2001||04:05 PM||||Audio cds are higher quality cds that will last a lot longer and play on more players, and will skip less when used a lot (like by a DJ).. thats what they want us to believe anyways  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||MinnGreenGT||11-01-2001||04:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>...I remember when the CD-writers first came out you had three choices of CD-R quality:  Gold, Silver, and the Blue/Green ones.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You are still correct on this one... the different qualities still exist, you just don't usually see any of the "good" ones in retail stores.  Check with someplace like <A HREF="http://www.mei-microcenter.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mei-microcenter.com</A>  under media- they've got quite a large selection.||||reg||1
Z||000003||avengador1||11-01-2001||04:49 PM||||Try <A HREF="http://www.techtv.com" TARGET=_blank>www.techtv.com</A>  they did a whole article on this a couple of months ago. Look at their archives in their TV shows "call for help" or "the screensavers".<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||AutoXdbdragSE||11-01-2001||05:20 PM||||The way the sony rep told us at best buy is that the Audio ones have a code in them that alows them to be burnt by home audio burners. otherwise they are the same as computer ones.  and it is this, not quality that acounts for the price difference.  It is because a home audio burner is only gonna be used to copy music. so in a deal with the record companys all manufactures pay Xamount of money. they make it off the CDR's and the only way to force people to buy the audio ones is to make them the only ones that work in home audio burners.  PC burners don't need it because the thought is they are used more like floppy disks so they don't need this extra charge becaue that's not what they are used for.<P>or so i was told||||reg||1
Z||000005||Brandon86SE||11-01-2001||05:26 PM||||AutoBdbdragSE is right.  I work as a salesman in an electronics store, and sell these.  The ones marked audio are for use in home cd-burners.  (you don't see a lot of those now, but that is how burners first came out)  There is absolutely no reason to buy those discs if you are using a computer burner.<P>Brandon<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Reality||11-01-2001||06:16 PM||||I'll back it up. I am/was an electronics mgr for a retail chain. Like they said there is no reason to buy the ones marked audio. We sell two type one audio one general use the audio is 19.99 for 30 the general is 11.88 cept they are exactly the same CD-R same company everything. I called them one day and asked the girl that fianlly answered the phone had very little smart things to say but did say that they are the same, i guess what I'm trying to say is that there is no difference!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> have fun.<p>[This message has been edited by Reality (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000007||btoth||11-01-2001||08:53 PM||||Thanks for clearing that up!<P>And it's nice to know that you can still find quality products somewhere.  There is a MicroCenter on the East side of Cleveland... that's where I used to have to go to buy stuff for my Macs before CompUSA starting carrying what they do now.||||reg||6
Z||000008||Raydar||11-02-2001||11:27 AM||||Something else to consider, if this is important to you. <BR>If you have a Sony (don't know about other brands) DVD player, it will play commercial  CDs, but *won't* play computer generated CD-R music. Maybe buying the "music" blanks will fix this. Not a big deal for me, though.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Some mornings, it just isn't worth chewing through the restraints.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000009||beer_stud_21||11-02-2001||09:39 PM||||btoth,<P>Try this place: <A HREF="http://www.cdrexpress.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cdrexpress.com</A><P>I got gold Kodac CD-R's from here a few months back and they have worked excellent for me.  Havn't had a bad burn that wasn't my fault yet.  ||||reg||1
